					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/elogee-fishtruck" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4037075); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4037075&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795045" alt="Elogee FishTruck" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4037075)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/elogee-fishtruck" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4037075); return false;" >Elogee FishTruck</a></span> <span class="odate time_1587144431 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">17 Apr 2020 17:27</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4600998">
									

<p>I have to agree with <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/roget" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229263); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1229263&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1587146489" alt="Roget" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1229263)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/roget" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229263); return false;">Roget</a></span>, both in terms of the clinical tone and the ending. I'm not as enthralled with the blood perception effect as <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/roget" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229263); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1229263&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1587146489" alt="Roget" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1229263)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/roget" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229263); return false;">Roget</a></span>, but I do agree that it could be put to good effect with the Foundation utility thing. I'm not entirely sure a testing log is what this skip needs, but maybe you can make the utilization of the anomaly by the Foundation less overt and more gradual.</p>
<p>The description right now is, besides being at times clinical, describing things that don't really need to be described in a Description, and would be better served with a more imagery-focused addendum. I'm thinking a gas leak occurs, and the only person who detects the non-air-tight sealing is some random janitor who was exposed to the anomaly, and that's how the Foundation uses it. As of now, the description is a classic case of telling instead of showing, which can be fixed with a reordering and refocusing of events.</p>
<p>My suggestions are thus:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Remove unnecessary sentences:</strong> This is part of making this document more clinical. By getting rid of unnecessary sentences, then you can get to the good stuff faster. Sentences like &quot;Its physical qualities conform to that of a standard consumer-grade video tape, but it does not show signs of wear or deterioration that typically occurs with those products.&quot; are unnecessary, especially, since we have an image. We don't need the text to tell us its a standard video tape, we can see it. Don't tell us what makes it normal, tell us what makes it unique.</li>
<li><strong>Remove the testing logs:</strong> The testing logs try to turn this into a horror-piece when it doesn't need to be. Plus, it doesn't really make sense. How does the D-class die in the invisible blood yet, when people are sleeping in bed for hours, they're just fine? Right now, the tesing log is serving no favors. <strong>EDIT:</strong> I think I understand why it affected the D-class and not the personnel now, but my point still stands that it distracts from the more memorable element of the skip.</li>
<li><strong>Introduce the Foundation's utilization of the anomaly later:</strong> Right now, it's just sort of summarized in a quick addendum, and it's in the middle of the piece, but I'd rather something like that be the finisher. The Foundation using a blood tape to fix air-leaks is a lot more memorable than &quot;D-class dies in testing log&quot;. As I said above, make it an incident log that introduces a scenario where the blood tape would be necessary, and give a short finisher of how the Foundation uses the blood tape beyond that.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is somewhat close to being good, but as of now, it's a -1.</p>

